{"sourceSha256": "93217502e88e904c602b75c5989cbd1d3d068b9253ea2192a79f14ad5dfb9302", "text": "Dr Jack Thomas\nGastroenterologist\nDepartment of Gastroenterology\nCity Hospital\nMain Road\nStillwater\n\n21/02/2015\n\nDear Dr Thomas,\n\nRe: Mr. Patrick Newtown                           DOB 06/07/1989\n\nI am writing to refer Mr. Newtown, a 25-year-old accountant, whose symptoms and signs are suggestive of\ninflammatory bowel disease. Your assessment would be highly appreciated.\n\nMr. Newtown is single. Unfortunately, he is a smoker. Moreover, he has suffered from bilateral mild\nintermittent wrist pain during the previous 6 months. Regarding his family history, his uncle has been\nsuffering from Crohn’s disease.\n\nToday, Mr. Newtown presented with a 4-month history of mild diarrhea, mild lower quadrant abdominal\npain, lethargy and a loss of appetite. Additionally, he lost 3 kg from his weight. Apart from diffuse       Commented [AH1]: has lost 3kg\nabdominal tenderness, his examination was unremarkable. Consequently, some laboratory tests were\nrequested. The test results showed an increase in WCC, CRP and ESR levels, but RCC and Hb concentration\nwere low. Furthermore, the ordered faecal occult blood test was positive. Therefore, I suspected\ninflammatory bowel disease, for which the patient was advised to quit smoking and consult a\ngastroenterologist.\n\nIn light of the above, the patient is being referred for further management. Should you have any queries,\nplease do not hesitate to contact me.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\nDoctor\n\n\n\n                                     Report\n Word length                  179\n Comments                     The content is relevant and well structured. Good\n                              control of language is evident in the use of both\n                              simple and complex sentences. Some\n                              weak/unnecessary wording is evident.\n Estimated Grade               B+\n Advice                   